package workbench.functional.l03.demo;

import java.util.function.Supplier;

/**
 * Java twin of Retry.kt. The work comes in as a Supplier<T>. Supplier.get() can't throw checked
 * exceptions, so code that throws IOException needs Callable<T> and a `throws Exception` on retry.
 */
public class RetryJava {

    static final class FlakyIssuer {
        private int failuresLeft;
        int calls = 0;
        FlakyIssuer(int failuresLeft) { this.failuresLeft = failuresLeft; }

        String authorize(String paymentId) {
            calls++;
            if (failuresLeft-- > 0) throw new IllegalStateException("issuer timeout on call " + calls);
            return "auth_" + paymentId;
        }
    }

    // A higher-order method: the loop is written once, and the work comes in as a function.
    static <T> T retry(int times, Supplier<T> block) {
        RuntimeException last = null;
        for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= times; attempt++) {
            try {
                return block.get();
            } catch (RuntimeException e) {
                last = e;
                System.out.println("  attempt " + attempt + " failed: " + e.getMessage());
            }
        }
        throw new IllegalStateException("gave up after " + times + " attempts", last);
    }

    // Another one: run any block, report how long it took, hand back its result unchanged.
    static <T> T timed(String label, Supplier<T> block) {
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        try {
            return block.get();
        } finally {
            System.out.printf("  %s took %.2f ms%n", label, (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1e6);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FlakyIssuer issuer = new FlakyIssuer(2);
        String auth = timed("authorize with retry", () -> retry(3, () -> issuer.authorize("pay_42")));
        System.out.println("result: " + auth + " after " + issuer.calls + " calls");

        FlakyIssuer down = new FlakyIssuer(5);
        try {
            retry(3, () -> down.authorize("pay_43"));
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println("result: " + e.getMessage() + " (last cause: " + e.getCause().getMessage() + ")");
        }
    }
}

sections.body:
Higher-order works in the other direction too. A function can build another function and hand it back.

```java
static Function<Long, Long> feeFor(String country) {
    long bps = RATES_BPS.get(country);                   // looked up once
    return amountCents -> amountCents * bps / 10_000;
}

Function<Long, Long> dutch = feeFor("NL");
dutch.apply(100_000L);                                   // 1400
```

```kotlin
fun feeFor(country: String): (Long) -> Long {
    val bps = RATES_BPS.getValue(country)                // looked up once
    return { amountCents -> amountCents * bps / 10_000 }
}

val dutch = feeFor("NL")
dutch(100_000)                                           // 1400
```

`fee(country, amount)` needs two arguments. `feeFor(country)` takes the first one now and gives back a function that waits for the second. Fixing some of a function's arguments and getting back a function of the rest is called **partial application**.

It pays off when the first argument is known early and the second arrives many times. A settlement job knows each merchant's country at startup and then computes thousands of fees. `feeFor` looks the rate up once, and the closure it returns (lesson 2) keeps only the number it needs. It also moves failure to the right moment. An unknown country fails when you build the function, at startup, not on the first payment at 3 a.m.

**Currying** takes the same idea all the way: every function takes exactly one argument and returns a function for the next. A curried fee has the type `(String) -> (Long) -> Long`. The arrows group to the right, so read it as `(String) -> ((Long) -> Long)`.

```java
Function<String, Function<Long, Long>> curriedFee = country -> amount -> fee(country, amount);
curriedFee.apply("DE").apply(100_000L);                  // 1500
```

```kotlin
val curriedFee: (String) -> (Long) -> Long = { country -> { amount -> fee(country, amount) } }
curriedFee("DE")(100_000)                                // 1500
```

Haskell curries every function. In Kotlin, don't. The signatures get hard to read, the IDE hints get noisy, and Kotlin already covers what currying is for: a lambda that fixes one argument (`{ a: Long -> fee("US", a) }`), default and named arguments, and bound references like `merchant::fee`. Write a function that returns a function when you mean "configure now, run many times", like `feeFor`. That's the case that earns its keep.

code.src:
package workbench.functional.l03.demo

val RATES_BPS = mapOf("NL" to 140L, "DE" to 150L, "US" to 290L)   // basis points: 1 bp = 0.01 %

// Both arguments at once.
fun fee(country: String, amountCents: Long): Long = amountCents * RATES_BPS.getValue(country) / 10_000

// Partial application: fix the country now, get back a function of the amount.
fun feeFor(country: String): (Long) -> Long {
    val bps = RATES_BPS.getValue(country)                 // looked up once, right here
    return { amountCents -> amountCents * bps / 10_000 }
}

// Currying by hand: take one argument, return a function that takes the next one.
val curriedFee: (String) -> (Long) -> Long = { country -> { amountCents -> fee(country, amountCents) } }

fun main() {
    val dutch = feeFor("NL")
    println("feeFor(\"NL\")(100000)        = ${dutch(100_000)}")
    println("curriedFee(\"DE\")(100000)    = ${curriedFee("DE")(100_000)}")

    // Kotlin idioms that usually read better than a curried signature:
    val usFee = { amountCents: Long -> fee("US", amountCents) }       // a lambda that fixes one argument
    println("lambda that fixes US        = ${usFee(100_000)}")
    println("named arguments             = ${fee(amountCents = 100_000, country = "NL")}")

    val amounts = listOf(10_000L, 250_000L, 1_000_000L)
    println("amounts.map(dutch)          = ${amounts.map(dutch)}")
}

